---
// ===========================================================================
// examples.astro — the applications, running, for the reader who has not
// cloned anything.
//
// THIS PAGE USED TO BE A LIST OF COMMANDS AND PORT NUMBERS. That is useful to
// somebody who already has the repository open and useless to everybody else,
// which is most of the people who reach a documentation site — so the page
// advertised six things a reader could not see. Four of them are on this site
// now: the showcase has its own page, and the three below run here.
//
// THEY ARE THE EXAMPLES, NOT COPIES. `../demo/PlainExamples.tsx` imports the
// same files `npm run example:*` serves and `npm run test:types` compiles, so
// an example that stopped being what this page says would fail the build
// rather than sit here looking plausible. Every snippet below is lifted from
// one of those files for the same reason.
//
// TWO ARE STILL COMMANDS, and the page says which and why rather than leaving
// a reader to find out: `playground` needs zustand and `nested-arrays` needs
// react-hook-form, its resolver package, Formik and TanStack Form — five
// dependencies this site does not carry and should not take on to draw a page
// about somebody else's API.
// ===========================================================================
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import {
  ServerErrorsExample,
  TwoFormsExample,
  WizardExample,
} from "../demo/PlainExamples.tsx";

const serverErrors = `
await form.submit(async (root) => {
  const answer = await createAccount(root);
  if (!answer.ok) form.adoptIssues(answer.issues);   // inside the handler
});
`.trim();

const sharedLeaf = `
// shared-fields.tsx imports neither schema, neither adapter and no handle.
function Text({ at, label }: { at: FormPathTo<string>; label: string }) {
  const field = useField(at);
  return (
    <label>
      <span>{label}</span>
      <input {...field.inputProps} />
      <span {...field.errorProps}>{field.issues[0]?.message}</span>
    </label>
  );
}

<Text at="customer:owner.email" label="Email" />
<Text at="admin:owner.email"    label="Owner email" />
`.trim();

const wizardProvider = `
// This subtree draws one step on purpose, and says so. Without it the
// coverage report names the fields of every step not on screen.
<FormProvider form={form} partial>
`.trim();

const vueField = `
import { FormProvider, useField } from "@maroonedog/waypoint/vue";

const Field = {
  setup() {
    const field = useField("form:owner.name");
    return () =>
      h("div", null, [
        h("label", field.labelProps, field.descriptor?.label),
        h("input", field.inputProps),
        h("p", field.errorProps, field.issues[0]?.message ?? ""),
      ]);
  },
};
`.trim();

/** Command, port, and where on this site it runs — one table rather than six
    sentences with a number buried in each. */
const COMMANDS = [
  { name: "showcase", port: 5179, here: "/showcase/" },
  { name: "server-errors", port: 5182, here: "#server-errors" },
  { name: "two-forms", port: 5183, here: "#two-forms" },
  { name: "wizard", port: 5184, here: "#wizard" },
  { name: "playground", port: 5178, here: undefined },
  { name: "nested-arrays", port: 5181, here: undefined },
];
---

<Base
  title="Examples — waypoint"
  description="Three applications running on this page — a server-error round trip, two forms sharing one component, and a wizard — plus the showcase, and the two that need libraries this site does not carry."
>
  <h1>Examples</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Six applications that run, and four of them run here. Each one exists to
    make a claim on this site checkable by typing into it rather than by
    believing a page.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    The three below are the example programs themselves, imported and mounted —
    not screenshots and not transcriptions. They are compiled by
    <code>npm run test:types</code> alongside the library, so one that stopped
    being what this page describes would fail the build. The biggest, a
    designed Material Design 3 screen, has a page of its own:{" "}
    <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/showcase/">Showcase</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    They are deliberately unstyled, which is why each sits in a frame with its
    own plain light palette. The showcase is the example that draws an
    interface; these are about one behaviour each and do not compete with it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="server-errors">server-errors</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    One round trip, and the two ways a complaint stops being true. The handler
    returns nothing: what the server said goes to <code>adoptIssues</code>,
    which is merged into every pass rather than written to a cell — so one list
    feeds the field, the count and the submit verdict, and they cannot come to
    disagree.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">Things to try.</strong> Put <code>ada</code>
    in the handle and a card number ending in <code>0</code>, then press. The
    first press is <em>not</em> refused — nothing was wrong when it was
    pressed, so the handler ran and the refusal arrived afterwards. Press again
    and it is refused before the handler runs at all. Now edit the handle: its
    message goes without anybody asking the server. Edit the card number and
    the decline stays, because nothing you can type is <em>about</em>
    <code>payment</code> — a path no input draws, which still blocks the submit
    and still appears in the summary, with no label, because the descriptor
    tree has never heard of it.
  </p>

  <div><ServerErrorsExample client:load /></div>

  <Code code={serverErrors} label="app: server-errors" lang="tsx" flush />

  <h2 id="two-forms">two-forms</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    Both forms declare <code>owner.email</code> and mean different things by
    it. The two inputs are the same component, which imports neither schema and
    takes one prop — an address that names its own form.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">Things to try.</strong> Type into one
    <code>owner.email</code> and watch the other stay where it was. Then empty
    both and press <em>Go to the first problem</em> on each screen: they land
    in different places, although both inputs really do carry
    <code>name="owner.email"</code> in the document. That is the one collision
    a qualified path does not solve — it happens in the DOM rather than in the
    types — and it is what <code>scopeProps</code> is for. The five spellings
    that must <em>not</em> compile live in <code>src/refusals.tsx</code>, each
    under <code>@ts-expect-error</code>, so the build fails if any of them ever
    starts compiling.
  </p>

  <div><TwoFormsExample client:load /></div>

  <Code code={sharedLeaf} label="app: two-forms" lang="tsx" flush />

  <h2 id="wizard">wizard</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    One form across three screens. Nothing is lifted, merged or stored per
    step, because every declared path was written before any component existed.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">Things to try.</strong> Type something on
    step 1, go to step 3, come back — it is still there, and it was never
    anywhere else. Leave step 2 empty and send it: the submit judges the whole
    root, so it is refused by name for a screen you were not looking at. The
    <code>partial</code> prop on the provider is why no coverage warning fires
    for the two steps that are off screen; without it the runtime would name
    their fields as undrawn, which is exactly what it should do when a field
    really was forgotten.
  </p>

  <div><WizardExample client:load /></div>

  <Code code={wizardProvider} label="app: wizard" lang="tsx" flush />

  <h2>The two that stay commands</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    These two draw other people&rsquo;s APIs and other people&rsquo;s stores.
    Putting them on this page would mean the documentation site taking on five
    dependencies it otherwise has no use for, so they stay commands — and this
    is what each one shows.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">playground</strong> —
    <code>npm run example:playground</code>. The one that is not a
    demonstration: a render counter on every field, and a store you can swap
    between the shipped one and a zustand one, so a claim about what a
    keystroke costs can be watched rather than read. Needs
    <code>zustand</code>.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">nested-arrays</strong> —
    <code>npm run example:nested-arrays</code>. The same
    <code>shipments[] → address&#123;&#125; → lines[]</code> shape written four
    times — waypoint, react-hook-form, Formik, TanStack Form — against one
    schema, with the same markup and the same behaviour. The line counts and
    path-mention counts it shows are derived from each file at run time rather
    than typed, and waypoint is not the shortest of the four, which is why line
    count is not the claim. Needs <code>react-hook-form</code>,
    <code>@hookform/resolvers</code>, <code>formik</code> and
    <code>@tanstack/react-form</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Running them from a clone</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    None of them needs a build: every example resolves the package to its
    <strong class="text-on-surface">source</strong>, so editing the runtime
    reloads the page. That is a reason to run one locally even for the four
    that are on this site — here they are built along with it, and there they
    answer an edit.
  </p>

  <!-- A table rather than a code block: `Code` takes ts or tsx, and a list of
       npm scripts highlighted as TypeScript is a block pretending to be
       something it is not.

       The scroller is the only thing left around the table. A command and a
       host:port are both unbreakable strings, so on a narrow screen this is
       the one block on the page that cannot be made to fit — and a table that
       widens the page takes the reading column with it. -->
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        {
          COMMANDS.map((one) => (
            <tr class="hairline">
              <td class="pr-6 font-mono whitespace-nowrap">
                npm run example:{one.name}
              </td>
              <td class="pr-6 font-mono text-on-surface-variant">
                localhost:{one.port}
              </td>
              <td class="text-on-surface-variant">
                {one.here === undefined ? (
                  "command only"
                ) : (
                  <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href={one.here}>
                    on this site
                  </a>
                )}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Vue</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    The examples above are React. The <code>./vue</code> entry reads the same
    registry — a Vue application writes the same
    <code>declare module "@maroonedog/waypoint"</code> a React one does — and
    the binding hands back the same four prop bags off the same descriptor.
    There is no Vue example application yet; what exists is the entry and its
    tests.
  </p>
  <Code code={vueField} label="@maroonedog/waypoint/vue" lang="ts" flush />

  <p class="caption">
    It is a first cut and smaller than <code>./react</code>. It has
    <code>useCreateForm</code>, <code>useForm</code>, <code>useField</code>,
    <code>useRows</code>, <code>useFormStatus</code>, <code>useCell</code>,
    <code>&lt;FormProvider&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;Field&gt;</code>,
    <code>&lt;FieldRows&gt;</code> and <code>decorateVNode</code> — the
    decorator ported, as a vnode rather than an element. What it does not have,
    named in its own entry file so a grep for one of them finds this sentence:
    <code>useUncontrolledField</code>, <code>useErrorSummary</code>,
    <code>useFieldValue</code>, <code>useFieldValues</code>,
    <code>useFieldIssues</code>, <code>useParticipation</code> and
    <code>&lt;AutoForm&gt;</code>.
  </p>
</Base>
